[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, there is a UV printer gold rush going on in the market right now . And almost all the UV printers I\'ve tested, whether it\'s the XTool Omni or the Morfo or the Ufimake, they all print on things, and they print really cool things. But there\'s one'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'printer that doesn\'t just print on things, it also creates the things themselves. And this is HeyGears G1X. And this machine is extremely interesting because they\'re actually using a'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'patent from 2001 that expired a few years ago. So in this video, I want to talk about four specific issues that can arise when you actually create something with UV ink, as opposed to just printing on top of it. Okay, let\'s'

[00:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'get to the point. Okay, big caveat. I don\'t have this machine in front of me right now, but I\'ve done a ton of patent research, as well as the thousands of comments and questions on Kickstarter that you have regarding this'

[00:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'machine. Additionally, I had hands-on experience working with competitors that exist. And something really interesting that I found when I first started researching this and found that there were patents that were expiring, is that'

[01:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I thought that it was because of this expiration that we started to see a lot of these machines. In particular, last year Anchor launched Ufim, which was, and I think still is, the biggest Kickstarter of all time, and it seemed like the floodgates'

[01:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'opened. And looking at it, the patent wasn\'t the reason for these machines, Ufim, it\'s actually all about Epson and the printheads . If you\'ve seen any of these videos, you know they all use'

[01:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Epson printheads. Some of the cheaper ones use the Epson 1080 , and then the better devices we\'re starting to see, including Hey Gears , use the Epson i3200 , which this guy has. And what\'s really interesting is that from 2008 to'

[01:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '2019, Epson no longer produced these printheads for external customers. So the only way to get these printheads was to simply buy an Epson machine. But starting in 2019,'

[01:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the situation changed, and Epson began supplying them to manufacturers. Namely the i3200, which has a lot of really cool things that we\'ll talk about here a little later, they started shipping it to'

[02:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'manufacturers in 2021. So this new influx of UV machines has a lot to do with the fact that they can now just buy the printheads directly from Epson, instead of having to manufacture them themselves, which is'

[02:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'extremely difficult. And then manufacturing and marketing, research and development, all that other stuff , you see a 4-5 year lag before these machines start coming to market. That explains these machines, but it\'s the patent that explains what\'s'

[02:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going on with HeyGears. And if you\'ve been involved in additive manufacturing for a while, this may sound familiar. 3D printers went through a similar process, where patents started to expire around 2009, and then we'

[02:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'saw a big influx of these 3D machines. And again, the same wave happened this time with resin processing machines, particularly Formlabs, where the patents died around 2014 and then the machines came along. And this story is'

[02:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'actually a little crazy because Formlabs started working on the machines and producing them even before the patents expired. So because of the lawsuits, they actually had to pay a commission on what they sold, returning it to the original patent owner while it was still'

[03:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'working. Um, this is all a bit crazy, and they were still first on the market, and they still exist today. Well, let\'s go back to 2026. Okay, but it\'s all printing on an object . In this case, it is ceramic onto which a'

[03:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'resin ink is applied , which is then cured with ultraviolet light and then applied in layers. Printing takes a while, and that brings us to the first problem HeyGears had to solve. And this has nothing to do'

[03:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with patents. This directly applies to the print head. Because I quickly discover that the print head is the most important thing, and the i3200 is a pretty incredible model. So, the i3200 can be'

[03:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'purchased with HeyGears. HeyGears offers several other versions of this machine. They have three different versions. They are loaded with three different types of ink. The first is regular UV'

[03:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ink. The second is DT ink, or something like fabric ink. This is kind of like what XTool does with their dual head design where there is UV ink and DT ink. But with HeyGears,'

[04:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you just choose any. And the last one is real 3D ink , which will have a different formula. And from what I\'m discovering, I believe that means this formula will be much thicker and'

[04:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'much more viscous, so it will be much stronger once it cures. Which brings us to the big advantage of the i3200. Essentially, it can push through a liquid that is much thicker than 1080. And without going into'

[04:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'detail, the print head on a UV printer works very differently than an inkjet printer you might find at home. A regular inkjet printer boils the ink, and when it boils, a small bubble pops up'

[04:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: ', and that\'s what gets onto the paper. These printers do it differently. They are called piezoelectric, and piezo comes from a Greek word meaning to press or squeeze. They have a crystal, actually'

[04:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'something like ceramic, that you apply voltage through and it essentially bends down, and then you have ink right underneath it, and when it bends, it pushes it out. So it\'s almost like a little piston that'

[05:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shoots them out. In the case of the i3200, you have about 3200 nozzles, that is, 3200 of these little nozzles that bend downwards. But considering the individual pressure it can deliver, the i3200 is'

[05:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'much stronger than the F1080. So you can get stronger ink. And Heygears does the same thing as Morpheus, where they mount the heater on the print head itself. And this is not from Epson, this is something'

[05:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they build on after the fact. So they are literally able to squeeze more performance out of the print head. And the i3200 has huge advantages in speed and performance, and that comes at a price. So'

[05:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: ', in this video I wanted to answer some of the questions on Kickstarter that I saw while doing this research. And just for your information, I think they actually put this up, uh, at Hey Gears, and they\'re the only one I think that'

[05:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'does this, where they\'re going to tell you how much it\'s going to cost to replace the head. Uh, and they say that replacing the head will cost $1,299, uh. And they want you to think of the head as a'

[06:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'consumable, a really expensive consumable given, but they say it will last about 2,100 hours, uh, and they\'ll give a 180-day warranty on the head itself. Well, that\'s more or less'

[06:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in line with what I see online, where for example a 1080 type printhead costs between $2 and $300 , while an i3200 will cost another thousand dollars. Um, so, uh, it definitely has a premium price tag of a thousand dollars, uh'

[06:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: ', but if you look at all the performance and all the features it has, it can definitely cost you a little bit more than that thousand dollars, especially if you sell things. And another thing I saw in the comments that people were asking about, uh,'

[06:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is it possible to switch between these, uh, printing systems? So, is it possible to switch between UV 3D resin or DTF, directly onto fabric? I\'ve actually seen the official position waver a bit , but from what I can'

[06:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tell, it\'s about as if you buy the car you want because replacing all of that would be pretty complicated. And technically you can replace it, but you\'d have to completely clean'

[07:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the lines, flush them, and then reintroduce ink into the system. This is one of the main reasons why the xTool Omni actually has two print heads inside, so you can use DT and UV at the same time. Hmm, they don\'t use'

[07:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'both of those printheads at the same time. As far as I can tell, the whole reason for this is so you can have the convenience of using multiple ink types and still run the system itself. And one unique thing that HeyGears does is that'

[07:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'essentially all of this ink is internal to the system. At Morpho they are just great here. And in XTool they are right here, on the back panel. And then you can do what I have right behind me, you can see it. Also located at'

[07:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the back. HeyGears actually has a separate tank. I think it\'s an additional tank that\'s nearby. Because you\'ll end up using significantly more ink when you\'re fully creating a 3D model compared to'

[07:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just a thin print, ahem, or even a small textured print . Instead of using a ribbon cable to connect the print head to the electronics, motherboard, and everything else, they actually use'

[08:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fiber optic cable because they know you\'re going to have to bend it back and forth thousands and thousands of times. Potentially even for one print. So they\'re developing a way to use this as something more like a regular 3D printer that you'

[08:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'set up and it runs for hours to be able to print. And we\'ll move on to time in a minute. And one more question about the print head, since we\'re talking about the comments on Kickstarter, that was probably the most surprising one'

[08:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: ', and it might change in the future, but you definitely have a simple machine. I mean , it\'s constantly pumping ink through the system. They say it\'s 3 to 5 ml in 8 hours, and then some of you have calculated that it'

[08:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'comes out to 450 ml per month or about $3 to $500 per year if it\'s idle. And all this system maintenance will be a huge task because the ink'

[08:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'clogs very easily. So they all approach it differently. In fact, I think Morfo does it best because the way they designed their inks says you don\'t need to run them through the system. We\'ll have to wait and see'

[09:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'how well it works. Okay, this is the printhead that solved the first problem you had, the ability to push very thick ink, and they use the i3200. The second problem is what this patent solves right here. In particular, the apparatus and'

[09:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'method of printing three-dimensional models. Specifically, how to print on something when there\'s nothing underneath . So, hang on. So if you\'ve done anything with 3D printing, you\'re very familiar with supports. These'

[09:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'benches are always a good example of how often when you print them from the bottom up you have to support something like the inside to have a surface to go back and print on. And actually FDM 3D printing'

[09:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'has become very useful because you don\'t really need to make a lot of supports. But with resin printing and ink, and its viscosity, you essentially need to maintain everything. So it\'s much rarer than even filament'

[09:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'when it comes out of a heated nozzle and extruder. So you can\'t have any overhang. And a lot of that initial patent work was basically around how to create these supports. And'

[10:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you really have no support for that. Um, the entire surface is covered, but they use different types of ink for what you don\'t want to see on the final object. That\'s why many of these prints from HeyGears that you\'ll see'

[10:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'look like they\'re completely covered by a clear sphere. And partly it\'s just for the end effect, but a lot of it is because they use water-soluble ink or water-soluble resin that can dissolve and'

[10:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reveal the final object in the middle. And that\'s more or less how they solve this problem. But resin printing, where you fill a vat with resin and then essentially print it upside down, um, still uses a UV lamp to'

[10:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cure that resin. It\'s a pretty similar, um, general process. But the main thing is that you are actually curing the resin inside the hopper itself. So it\'s kind of submerged, and you have a big screen on the bottom that directs the UV'

[10:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'light only to where it needs to cure. You will still have to use supports because, like gravity, everything hangs down, but that is not the case where the entire layer is cured by UV light. Also, you can\'t'

[11:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'use some of the tricks you can use with resin printing, like where you can get a cocoon where you have an outer shell but nothing inside. So in this case you save on material and potentially time. In'

[11:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this case, you can\'t do that because the layers must again be supported from below . So, with all that said , these 3D prints are great, but they take a lot of time. At least the ones we\'ve seen'

[11:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'published. HeyGears said you could do some things in a few hours, but all the machines I\'ve seen people testing online take 20 to 30 hours to print. But then you'

[11:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'actually have to dissolve that print in a bath if you want to remove those supports. The Strange Parts actually gave a tour of the factory, and they did a slow-motion film that showed that it took about 6-7 hours to dissolve the prints. So'

[11:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: ', considering all of the above, it will take a lot of time to print these 3D objects. And they won\'t be too massive. But just like how standard 3D printers have become much faster over the'

[12:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'years, we hope to see the same in this case. Okay, problem number three is more on the software side, because now you have this 3D object, but you can create it in the same color in different ways. What'

[12:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'makes it unique is that it is full-color on the outside. And the appearance is key because there is only a colored shell around it that is basically white inside. So it\'s almost like a jawed choke that you can'

[12:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just split in half and you see it\'s completely white with a thin colored shell on the outside. Now, even though the color part of it is small, that\'s where the big impact of this whole thing lies. And being able to determine'

[12:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'where to apply color is quite a complex problem, because if we\'re talking about standard 2D printing, the RGB image will be converted to a CYMK image so that the ink can be used. And then there will be'

[12:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'dithering, or essentially tiny specks of color that, when viewed from a sufficient distance, mix to produce all the different colors. I actually experimented with dithering quite a bit while trying to write my own'

[13:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'software for the laser side. So , just converting a grayscale image to grayscale... You need to do a lot of research to make it really complex. And that\'s before you even start adding'

[13:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'full color to it. But then take that and apply it to the entire object. And wrapping an image around a 3D object is nothing new. If you do any type of 3D modeling,'

[13:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this is a fairly common problem where you will often have two files. One will be the 3D model itself, i.e. the actual structure. And the second one will be, in essence, an image that can be wrapped. The difficulty is that these are not just pixels on a screen.'

[13:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You actually need to convert these colors into ink percentages. You really need to define, meaning you need to turn all those colors into tiny drops that mix together to create what you want. What makes it even more difficult is that the ink is not'

[14:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'completely opaque. Uh, a lot of the colors are mixed, almost like when painting with oil paints, and you can build up these layers of color and then overlay those colors through transparencies. This is what you have in 3D space.'

[14:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, depending on the angle you look at it from, you\'ll see a lot of different colors building up in 3D. So this seems like a pretty nasty, uh, software problem . And this is a problem that'

[14:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'big players like Stratasys still have a patent on, well into the 2030s. And that\'s probably the most important thing that I think HeyGears was able to do, is more or less develop their own software,'

[14:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'algorithms and processes to be able to compute this and then print it and still get really, really good results, as they showed. Well, one of them is your vertical resolution, so the magnification will be'

[14:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'about half what your resolution would be if it were just a standard photo. And I think they actually have statistics on this on Kickstarter, which I\'ll post below. But just know that the resolution won\'t be as accurate.'

[15:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'That is, if you look at your object from the side, its resolution will be much lower than what you do from top to bottom. And you also see the same thing with a regular 3D printer, but the thing is, with regular 3D printing, there\'s also no'

[15:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'color blur where it really stands out if it\'s a very flat vertical image that you\'re working with. Okay, and the last problem they had to solve is that you can\'t really see when you print flat. Uh, if you can imagine'

[15:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'all 3200 nozzles shooting ink, hmm, there\'s a very good chance that not every drop of ink will be exactly the same. That is, the surface will not be completely flat. It\'s usually not that'

[15:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'big of a problem , if you print like this, um, it just looks a little textured, and you can still put, um, a clear coat on top and it\'ll level itself out and be ready to go. But if you\'re 3D printing, you need'

[15:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'those layers to be perfectly even so that when it turns around and drops the next layer, um, it has somewhere to go. Or it won\'t shrink because it\'s already too tall. And so there'

[16:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'was actually another patent that helped solve this problem, where they more or less used something like an improved squeegee to remove a tiny layer of ink to get a really smooth surface. Now you\'re wasting a lot of'

[16:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ink, especially if it\'s all color, you can have those inks bleeding into each other. And that\'s exactly what many professional machines have done over the years. This patent expires in 2023, so technically they'

[16:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'could use it, but that\'s not the way to solve this problem. Instead, they say they use a proprietary ink formula that is much more self-leveling. So, whether it comes out much more'

[16:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'evenly, falls more evenly, flattens out after landing, something like that. And they also provide a lidar sensor that can more or less figure out that layer and check it, and then automatically calibrate the next pass based on what'

[16:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they see. So, a little chemistry and a little software give you a unique solution from HeyGears. They proposed several different unique solutions. And then you might wonder if'

[17:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they patented their solutions for this machine. As far as I can tell, no. But they have made a big contribution to where I think the future of full-color 3D printing is headed. And they have documents for this since'

[17:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '2024. We won\'t go into detail in this video, but let\'s just say you don\'t have to worry about the Epson, and it\'s much more like a traditional resin-based printer. Okay, I\'d like to know what questions you have about this machine. I\'m going to'

[17:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'post a big comparison video that breaks down the stats of all these machines that exist, the ones I\'ve had personal experience with, and the stats I can check online. So, let me know what you would like answered and I will do my best to take it into account in the'

[17:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'future. Okay, until next time, go make or break something in your workshop. See you later, guys.'
